\[h_{n} = \frac{60}{72}(-.0006) = -.00049\]

Thus

\[Q(84|72) = Q(1.0046 -.0005) = Q(1.0041) = 1 - P(1.0041) = .15766\]

### Square root approximation 26.4.13

Using the square root approximation we have \(Q(84|72) = Q(\bar{x})\) where

\[x = \sqrt{2(84)} - \sqrt{2(72)} - 1 = 1.0032.\]

This results in

\[Q(84|72) = Q(1.0032) = 1 - P(1.0032) = .15788\]

The value correct to 6D is \(Q(84|72) = .157653\). Generally the improved cube root approximation will be correct with a maximum error of a few units in the fifth decimal and is recommended for calculations which are outside the range of Table 26.7.

**Example 13.** Find the value of x\({}^{2}\) for which \(Q(\chi^{2}|144) = .01\).

Since \(v = 144\) is outside the range of Table 26.8, we can compute it by using (1) the Cornish-Fisher asymptotic expansion 26.2.50, for x\({}^{2}\), (2) the cube approximation 26.4.17, (3) the improved cube approximation 26.4.18, or (4) the square approximation 26.4.16. We shall compute the value by all four methods.

**1. Cornish-Fisher asymptotic expansion 26.2.50**

The Cornish-Fisher asymptotic expansion for x\({}^{2}\) with \(v = 144\) can be written as

\[\chi^{2} \sim 144 + 12\sqrt{2}x + 4h_{i}(x) + \frac{4\sqrt{2}}{12}\left[3h_{i}(x) + 2h_{i1}(x) \right] + \frac{8}{12^{2}}\left[6h_{i}(x) + 3h_{i2}(x) + 2h_{i1}(x) \right] + 1\frac{16\sqrt{2}}{12^{3}}\left[30h_{i}(x) \right.\] \[\left.+ 9h_{i2}(x) + 12h_{i3}(x) + 6h_{i12}(x) + 4h_{i11}(x) \right]\]

Hence using the auxiliary table following 26.2.51 with \(p = .01\) we have

\[\begin{array}{l}144.0000\\ 39.4794\\ 2.9413\\ --.0242\\ -.0019\\ +.0002\end{array}\]

## 4. Square root approximation 26.4.13

Using the square root approximation we have \(Q(84|72) = Q(\bar{x})\) where

\[x^{2} = \sqrt{2(84)} - \sqrt{2(72)} - 1 = 1.0032.\]

This results in

